{"name":"Arabella","hex":"#82acc4","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Arabella","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Latin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Arabella"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arabella","blocks":[{"text":"Arabella, Op. 79, is a lyric comedy, or opera, in three acts by Richard Strauss to a German libretto by Hugo von Hofmannsthal, their sixth and last operatic collaboration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Ferdinand_Schmutzer_-_Richard_Strauss%2C_1922.jpg/330px-Ferdinand_Schmutzer_-_Richard_Strauss%2C_1922.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabella","wikidata":"Q390779"},{"title":"Arabella (Arctic Monkeys song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Arabella\" is a song by English rock band Arctic Monkeys from their fifth studio album, AM (2013). The song was confirmed as the album's fifth single by the band and their label Domino and impacted radio in Italy on 28 January 2014 and the United Kingdom on 10 March 2014. A physical 7\" vinyl single was also planned for release on 28 March 2014, but was ultimately cancelled."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7e/Arctic_Monkeys_-_Arabella.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabella_(Arctic_Monkeys_song)","wikidata":"Q16160797"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"Arabella":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-Arabella.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/ae/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-Arabella.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-Arabella.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/ae